  "findings": [
    "Over 19 covered months the split of income is 53 per cent needs, 28 per cent wants and 26 per cent savings, against the 50/30/20 rule.",
    "Wants run SGD 806.30 a month against 1,512.75 of billed needs, a ratio of 0.53 to 1.",
    "Above the rule: savings."
  ],
  "caveats": [
    "The three shares are of income and do not add to one: what is left is what stayed in the bank. Wants are 28 per cent of income and 35 per cent of spending, and only the second is a split of what was spent.",
    "Every Spend category carries a Need class, so `unclassified` is zero; it is kept in the table because a new category with no Need class would land there rather than quietly joining needs.",
    "Shares are of income, not of take-home pay: the record has no payslip in it, so gross and net cannot be told apart.",
    "Needs read low in any month a bill was paid from an account this record does not hold, so a dip in the needs share is worth checking against the statements before it is read as saving.",
    "Savings is the Investment and Savings need classes on Transfer-bucket categories, which is the Tiger Brokers DCA; money simply left in the bank is not counted as saved."
  ]
